WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.776
Why do some islands have many species and others very few?

00:00:03.776 --> 00:00:06.376
This is the question of island biogeography.

00:00:07.476 --> 00:00:08.887
Robert MacArthur and E.

00:00:08.887 --> 00:00:09.010
O.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:13.184
Wilson treated an island's species number as a balance of two rates.

00:00:13.184 --> 00:00:18.524
Immigration falls as the island fills up, because fewer new species are left to arrive.

00:00:18.524 --> 00:00:22.575
Extinction rises, because more species compete for the same space.

00:00:22.575 --> 00:00:26.258
Where the two curves cross, the number of species is stable.

00:00:26.258 --> 00:00:30.125
Watch what happens as the island gets bigger: extinction falls,

00:00:30.125 --> 00:00:32.518
and the equilibrium moves to the right.

00:00:33.618 --> 00:00:38.968
The theory explains a pattern seen on islands everywhere: the species–area relationship.

00:00:38.968 --> 00:00:43.649
On islands, a tenfold increase in area roughly doubles the number of species.

00:00:44.749 --> 00:00:46.965
The General Dynamic Model adds time.

00:00:46.965 --> 00:00:51.582
A volcanic island is born, grows in area and height, then erodes and sinks.

00:00:51.582 --> 00:00:54.167
Species richness and the number of unique,

00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:57.738
endemic species rise and then fall over the island's life.

00:00:57.738 --> 00:01:01.185
Surtsey, already shrinking only decades after its birth,

00:01:01.185 --> 00:01:03.708
is the fastest-moving test of this model.

00:01:04.808 --> 00:01:10.183
A third idea, the island rule, says that on islands small animals tend to evolve larger,

00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:11.771
and large animals smaller.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:15.008
Treat all three theories as models to test, not laws.

00:01:15.008 --> 00:01:20.016
Real islands get extra nutrients from seabirds, receive species carried by people,

00:01:20.016 --> 00:01:22.398
and sometimes were once joined to land.
